Exercise 1.26 defines, for a commutative ring \(A\) with unit and for \(\Delta\) the finite subsets of \(A\), the neighbourhood \(I(F)=\{G\in\Delta\mid F\subseteq\langle G\rangle\}\), asks to prove that the sets \(I(F)\) form a neighbourhood system whose domain is isomorphic to the ideals of \(A\) ordered by inclusion, and asks what remains if every finite \(F\) with \(I(F)=I(\{1\})\) is removed from \(\Delta\). `IFamily` is that set of finite \(G\). `IFamily_inter` is \(I(F_1)\cap I(F_2)=I(F_1\cup F_2)\), `IFamily_subset_iff` is \(I(F_1)\subseteq I(F_2)\) if and only if \(F_2\) lies in the ideal generated by \(F_1\), and `IFamily_eq_of_span_eq` with `span_eq_of_IFamily_eq` make equal generated ideals the same condition as equal neighbourhoods. `ringSystem` takes the sets \(I(F)\) as neighbourhoods on the finite subsets, with master \(I(\emptyset)\), and its intersection law returns \(I(F_1\cup F_2)\) for every pair, so the family is closed under all binary intersections; the carrier is assumed to have decidable equality so those finite-set unions are formed. `idealOf` sends a filter to \(\{a\mid I(\{a\})\in x\}\), obtaining zero by upward closure from the master, a sum by rewriting the intersection of the singleton neighbourhoods as \(I(\{a,b\})\) and closing upward, and a scalar multiple by upward closure along \(I(\{a\})\subseteq I(\{c\cdot a\})\). `ofIdeal` sends an ideal back to the filter of those \(I(F)\) whose generated ideal lies in it. `mem_IFamily_of_forall` puts \(I(F)\) into a filter that already contains every singleton neighbourhood of a member of \(F\), and that lemma is the inductive step of `ofIdeal_idealOf`; `idealOf_ofIdeal` is the other round trip, a singleton span contained in the ideal. `ringIso` is the order isomorphism, reflecting inclusion of ideals as approximation of filters. The deletion of the finite sets \(F\) with \(I(F)=I(\{1\})\) is not treated. The bundled `example_126` is only `IFamily_eq_of_span_eq`. **Theorem:** partial — the neighbourhood system and the isomorphism with the ideal poset are proved, and the effect of deleting those tokens is not. **Proof:** partial — the intersection identity, the ideal axioms, and the two round trips with order reflection are the argument for the isomorphism; the exclusion question has no argument.
